/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-18-       [priority(40)]
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-19-
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-20-  rule <k> AugAssign(Name(X:String), OP:String, V:Val) => .K ... </k>
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-21-       <env> L:Int </env>
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k:22:       <scopes> ... L |-> scope(M:Map => M [ X <- applyBin(OP, {M[X]}:>Val, V) ], _) ... </scopes>
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-23-       requires X in_keys(M)
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k:24:  // `lst += [..]` where lst is a heap ref: the generic rule leaves applyBin(OP, ref, V) — but the
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-25-  // ref-deref preemption is BinOp-level (operators.k), so applyBin never derefs and it sticks. Route
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-26-  // the ref case through BinOp so the deref + list-concat + #alloc path fires (result is a fresh ref).
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-27-  rule <k> AugAssign(Name(X:String), OP:String, V:Val) => Assign(Name(X), BinOp(OP, Name(X), V)) ... </k>
/reference/k-proof/reference-semantics/semantics/controls.k-28-       <env> L:Int </env>
